  "summary": "EFF's analysis of Flock Safety ALPR search logs obtained through public records requests found officers at dozens of agencies routinely entering nonsensical or joke justifications (e.g., 'LOL', 'LMAO', 'idk', 'TBD', insults, or keyboard mashing) when searching license plate data. The piece catalogs examples across many departments, reports agency responses, and criticizes Flock's shift to a dropdown menu as reducing transparency, urging warrant requirements and stricter limits.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Police are routinely searching the Flock database without providing any legitimate justification, making a mockery of our civil liberties by logging reasons like \"LOL\" ... \"LMAO\" ... \"sexy,\" and \"idk\" ... to access sensitive ALPR location data.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "More than 30 agencies ran more than 6,300 searches with \"TBD\" (short for \"To Be Determined\") as the \"reason.\"",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "Replacing the requirement to articulate the reason for the search with one-click searches is a loss for transparency",
      "paragraph_idx": 27
    },
    {
      "quote": "If police want the power to track a person's movements, they must be required to convince a judge with evidence and probable cause.",
      "paragraph_idx": 32
    }
  ],
